now suppose i want to add a link from where a student can buy package where they get option to select among mocks, descriptive and study notes (checkbox multiple selection) and they pay only for what they select, and this purchase overrides the single topic lock for them, what changes will it require?

To implement a dynamic bundle/package purchase system where students can mix and match items (mock tests, descriptive papers, and study notes) via checkboxes and pay a custom calculated price—with these bulk purchases automatically overriding single-item locks—you will need to make changes across your frontend UI, backend purchase ledger, and access gate.

Here are the complete architecture changes required:
1. Frontend UI Changes (public/js/ or index pages)

You will need to create a new "Custom Package Builder" screen or modal where students can view all available items with checkboxes.

    Dynamic Price Calculation: As the student checks/unchecks items (e.g., selecting 2 mock tests at ₹49 each and 1 study note at ₹49), JavaScript dynamically sums up the total price in real-time.

    Payload Generation: When they click "Buy Selected Package", your frontend groups all selected item IDs into an array (e.g., ['mock_genetics_01', 'PRO_notes_bio.pdf']) and passes them to a new custom checkout route.

2. Backend Changes (server.js)
A. New Multi-Item Order Route

Instead of buying a single item, you need an endpoint that accepts a list of item IDs, calculates the total price on the server (preventing client-side price tampering), and creates a single Razorpay order for the combined sum.

Add this new endpoint to server.js:
JavaScript

app.post('/api/create-bundle-order', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        if (!razorpay) return res.status(503).json({ error: 'Payments are currently unavailable.' });
        const authCheck = await verifyAuthToken(extractAuthToken(req));
        if (!authCheck.valid) return res.status(401).json({ error: authCheck.error });

        const { itemIds } = req.body;
        if (!Array.isArray(itemIds) || itemIds.length === 0) {
            return res.status(400).json({ error: 'No items selected.' });
        }

        let calculatedTotal = 0;

        // Server-side price calculation for each item in the bundle
        for (const itemId of itemIds) {
            const cleanId = path.basename(String(itemId)).trim();
            let itemPrice = 49; // default fallback

            if (cleanId.toUpperCase().endsWith('.PDF')) {
                itemPrice = parsePdfMetadata(cleanId).price || 49;
            } else {
                const quizPath = resolveSafeQuizPath(cleanId);
                if (quizPath) {
                    const content = getCachedQuizContent(quizPath);
                    itemPrice = Number(content.price) > 0 ? Number(content.price) : 49;
                }
            }
            calculatedTotal += itemPrice;
        }

        const order = await razorpay.orders.create({
            amount: Math.round(calculatedTotal * 100), // Total in paisa
            currency: "INR",
            receipt: `rcpt_bundle_${Date.now().toString().slice(-8)}`
        });

        res.json({ success: true, orderId: order.id, amount: order.amount, keyId: process.env.RAZORPAY_KEY_ID, itemIds });
    } catch (err) {
        console.error('Bundle order error:', err);
        res.status(500).json({ error: 'Bundle order creation failed.' });
    }
});

B. Multi-Item Payment Verification Route

When payment succeeds, your server needs to loop through the array of items and grant ownership for every selected item individually to the student's ledger.

Add this verification endpoint:
JavaScript

app.post('/api/verify-bundle-payment', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const authCheck = await verifyAuthToken(extractAuthToken(req));
        if (!authCheck.valid) return res.status(401).json({ error: authCheck.error });

        const { razorpay_order_id, razorpay_payment_id, razorpay_signature, itemIds } = req.body;
        if (!razorpay_order_id || !razorpay_payment_id || !razorpay_signature || !Array.isArray(itemIds)) {
            return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Missing bundle payment details.' });
        }

        const body = razorpay_order_id + "|" + razorpay_payment_id;
        const expectedSignature = crypto
            .createHmac('sha256', process.env.RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET || '')
            .update(body.toString())
            .digest('hex');

        if (safeCompareHex(expectedSignature, razorpay_signature)) {
            const tenYearsSec = 60 * 60 * 24 * 365 * 10;
            
            // Grant ownership for each item in the bundle package
            for (const itemId of itemIds) {
                await stateStore.set(`user:purchases:${authCheck.email}:${itemId}`, '1', tenYearsSec);
                await stateStore.sadd(`user:purchases:${authCheck.email}`, itemId);
            }

            res.json({ success: true });
        } else {
            res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid payment signature.' });
        }
    } catch (err) {
        console.error('Bundle verification error:', err);
        res.status(500).json({ error: 'Bundle payment verification failed.' });
    }
});

3. How This Automatically Overrides Single Topic Locks

Because of how we structured your Zero-Trust Universal Access Gate (checkContentAccess), no changes are required to the access logic itself!

Here is why it works automatically:

    When a student buys items individually or via a custom bundle, the backend writes the exact same keys to Redis (user:purchases:<email>:<itemID> and the set user:purchases:<email>).

    When the student subsequently attempts to open a mock test, study note, or descriptive paper, checkContentAccess checks Step 3 (the Purchase Ledger).

    Since the bundle payment script successfully registered those item IDs into the student's purchase ledger, the access gate instantly recognizes them as owned, bypassing both single-topic locks and paywalls seamlessly.

