when i extracted the downloaded zip file from github my windows defender immediately alerted me about a threat mentioning something like 'trozan...', what is it?

That alert typically means Windows Defender detected a file signature, pattern, or piece of code inside the downloaded zip file that matches known malware catalogs (specifically a Trojan horse).

When you download a zip archive directly from a GitHub repository—especially one containing custom scripts, compiled binaries, or unpackaged code—it is very common for Windows Defender to flag it falsely (a "false positive") if it contains unsigned executable files, batch scripts, or standard web tools that trigger heuristic scans.
What You Should Do Next

    Do not run or extract any executables (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .ps1) from that zip until you verify its source.

    Check the Repository Source: If you downloaded the repository from an official, trusted developer source (such as your own project repository or a widely known open-source tool), it is almost certainly a false positive caused by an unsigned build script or utility.

    Verify via VirusTotal: You can upload the zip file or individual suspicious files to VirusTotal to scan them against dozens of major antivirus engines simultaneously. If only one or two obscure vendors flag it, it is a false positive. If multiple major engines flag it, delete the file immediately.

is this a sign that an attacker has planted this trojan horse in my code present in github repository

No, this is almost certainly not a sign that an attacker has compromised your GitHub repository or planted malicious code.

What you experienced is a very common false positive triggered by Windows Defender (or other local antivirus software) when downloading code archives directly from GitHub.
Why Did Windows Defender Flag It?

    Unsigned Files and Scripts: When you download a raw ZIP archive of a repository (especially one containing custom Node.js modules, build scripts, or packaged tools like the ones you've been building for Pioneers Prep), the files are entirely uncompiled and lack digital signatures from a recognized software vendor. Antivirus heuristics often treat unsigned scripts or unfamiliar ZIP structures with caution.

    Heuristics and Pattern Matching: Windows Defender scans files using automated pattern matching. If your codebase contains utility functions, data encoding snippets (like custom string manipulation or XOR ciphers used for data protection), or network communication modules (like fetch routines or local socket connections), automated scanners can easily misidentify them as suspicious behaviors associated with trojans.

    No External Compromise Required: This flag happens completely locally on your machine after download. It does not mean someone hacked your GitHub account, modified your remote repository, or injected a backdoor into your cloud code.

How to Verify It's Safe

If you want to double-check the files before proceeding, you can take these steps:

    Check your GitHub Commit History: Log into GitHub and check the Commits tab of your repository. If you don't see any unfamiliar code changes, commits, or pull requests made by accounts other than yours, your repository code has not been altered by an external party.

    Scan via VirusTotal: You can upload the specific ZIP file or individual flagged files to VirusTotal. It will run them through dozens of different antivirus engines simultaneously. If only Windows Defender flagged it (or it comes back clean across almost all engines), it is a classic false positive.

