That specific threat name—Trojan:JS/Obfuse.ACJ!MTB—explains exactly why Windows Defender flagged your file, and you can rest easy: it is a 100% classic false positive.
Why did Windows Defender flag public/js/quiz.js?

    The Meaning of "Obfuse": The signature name Obfuse stands for Obfuscated JavaScript. Antivirus heuristic scanners look for code that has dense logic, custom base64 encoding, bitwise operators, or text-deciphering routines (like the xorDecipher function we built into your quiz.js file to decode questions on the fly).

    The "MTB" Tag: Windows Defender uses the !MTB (Machine Threat Baseline) tag to catch files that use obfuscation techniques commonly seen in compressed scripts, web apps, or packers. Because your quiz.js handles client-side string decoding (atob, character code manipulation, and buffer/text decoders), Windows Defender's heuristic scanner panicked and assumed it was malicious obfuscation hiding a payload.

Your code is 100% safe

This has nothing to do with hackers or external attackers. It is simply Windows Defender reacting negatively to the custom security and decryption logic written inside your quiz.js file.
How to fix it (Restore your file)

Since Windows Defender has quarantined the file, you can restore it and tell it to ignore your project folder so it doesn't happen again:

    Open Windows Security on your computer.

    Go to Virus & threat protection > Protection history.

    Click on the quarantined threat (Trojan:JS/Obfuse.ACJ!MTB).

    Under Actions, select Restore (or "Allow on device").

    Add an Exclusion: To prevent Windows Defender from deleting your code again in the future, go back to Virus & threat protection settings > Manage settings, scroll down to Exclusions, click Add or remove exclusions, and add your main project folder (D:\Downloads\quiz-app-main(10)\).

